[Music]  i get the question all the time  wasn't it wonderful in 1947 jackie  robinson breaks the color barrier  opens up major league baseball to  everyone my answer has to be  no it wasn't it was actually more  difficult for african-american  ballplayers  many talented black players got phased  out  racism ended my professional career  the primary reason for the end of the  negro leagues  is integration what we saw with the  negro leagues  and particularly those negro league  teams that were  in close proximity to major league teams  they died in almost instant death  because that black fan base left to go  see jackie  to go see larry dobey we're trying to  get to the major leagues and once that  was an option  these segregated teams and leagues were  abandoned completely  in the 50s the indianapolis clowns  dropped out of the negro american league  now all the great players they've been  signed a major league  folks that own the teams and that were  very smart individuals they knew  baseball  but they also knew business did pollock  abe saperstein  who both had business interests in the  harlem globetrotters  hired women to play for the indianapolis  clowns  surprising that i've seen women playing  in any eager leagues  they could play tony stone was the first  woman to play for the indianapolis  clowns  she's followed by mimi peanut johnson  and  connie morgan who competed against the  men in the 1950s  was it a promotional gimmick yes was it  successful  yes they were trying to get people to  the ballparks  and at the same time they're selling  their best players  to the major leagues trying to fill the  role of all these lost ball players  and it really doesn't work and if it did  work for some teams it only worked  maybe four or five years unfortunately  just because  jackie robinson integrated baseball that  didn't  change the fact that it was going to be  very difficult for young black ball  players  before i leave this earth somebody out  there know the answer  why is it at that time they were  drafting  black men to go fight and die  for this country but they didn't want us  to play baseball  for this country black men  helped build this country history  isn't always necessarily pretty and  it's not always as glamorous as people  would like it to be  there were a lot of guys that never got  the opportunity  to go beyond minor league baseball  there's no question that there was a  quota system in place  when that first group of black players  transitioned into the major leagues  i had the fortune of knowing a gentleman  by the name of bobby break  bobby was the manager for the pittsburgh  pirates at the time  he was very honest with me he said we  had a quota system back in those days  you couldn't have more than four black  ball players on your team in the story  and that was pushing it i wanted to make  it to the major league  i thought i was going to make it with my  speed and hidden  because i could hit 1950s jake gets  signed by the brooklyn dodgers  organization  they sent him to minor league training  camp he has a phenomenal  training camp leading hitter on the team  starting outfielder  they make the cuts for the season jake's  one of the first ones that get cut  why do you cut the best baseball player  on your team  that's what hurt me so bad when they cut  me  for a white kid then 264 and i'm about  to  clean up i was serious about the game  but it's one of those things pedro  played everywhere  you can play professional baseball he  played in the negro leagues he played  in minor league baseball he played in  canada he played dominican republic he  played in mexico  i don't have any regrets of what  happened i thought i'd think to myself  you know that i that i could have played  and those who  played around me knew that i could when  i got in professional baseball  i didn't realize just how bad  racism was ernest got to play  in the st louis cardinals organization  the next year  i was traded to the kansas city  that's when racism set in  my own teammate they didn't even want me  to sit on the bench water  now the people understand i could take  that  and keep walking but my whole teammate  it bothered me because i felt that  baseball for everybody  they figured i'll get discouraged and  quit  but i showed them i could play anywhere  on a baseball field so what they started  doing  they would make up excuses to make sure  no other team signed you they said  you play your music too loud man  i wasn't making enough money to buy  stereo  radio if america  truly appreciated the greatest athletes  there wouldn't have been  a negro league there wouldn't have been  an all-white major league  and then we would have truly seen the  greatest baseball players of all time  those aren't the truly greatest numbers  because they didn't include the truly  greatest players  the husband said oh we don't have the  stats but i  found scorebooks i found in some of the  black newspapers they kept scoring a  tally  since 1947 think of all the records that  have fallen  related to african-american baseball  players that didn't have to start in  1947  it could have started in 1907 it could  have started  in 1887. so baseball history would be  changed  but we will never know how much  [Music]  but this story the crux of it looks at  a story that is about the importance of  economic empowerment it is a story about  an  unprecedented level of leadership that  emerged  as a result of the formation of these  leagues  black coaches black owners black  executives  they were done once the negro leagues  died and we don't hear about them what  what would it mean  if black kids didn't just want to be the  next satchel page or josh gibson but  they wanted to be the next gus greenlee  or the next epham manly one of the  things i  would like to see happen is that it  would be in  schools because the negro league  accomplished a lot of things  other than just baseball the negro  league  made not only black history american  history  and all of that is wrapped around this  amazing story of these very courageous  athletes who as i like to say forged a  glorious history  in the midst of an inglorious time in  american history  to play the game they love we don't want  people to forget about josh gibson and i  was just josh gibson people forget about  the negro leagues  so what we're trying to do is keep this  legacy alive is through the job skips  and foundations making sure that the  youth is educated  on the negro leagues as well as making  sure they get the right education to  further the education so we have a job  skipping scholarships we have a job  curriculum it teaches kids the business  side of sports sports media sports law  sports marketing for sales  [Music]  when we built the negro leagues baseball  museum 30 years ago  we knew then that it was literally a  race against time  that those who had created this history  it wasn't a matter of if it was simply a  matter when we were going to lose them  and that it was so vitally important  that their story  and their legacy would play on long  after  there were no negro league players to  attest  to the greatness of these leagues a lot  of the researchers are getting older  like myself  you know i'm 70 years old and it's  tremendously gratifying to me that  there's someone there to carry on this  legacy  as we celebrate the 100th anniversary of  the negro leagues major league baseball  has made a commitment to also honor the  new colleagues  back in august they all were the  centennial patch there's been  several major league baseball teams who  recognize january league baseball  players  milwaukee brewers has celebrated the  negro leagues pittsburgh pirates  atlanta braves kansas city royals  chicago  white sox detroit tigers by either  bringing some of these players back to  honor them or just having some kind of  exhibit to showcase their fans  right now i'm trying to get young kids  back interested in baseball i coach  little league team i talk to kids  [Music]  what the negro leagues teaches us is  quite simple in this  country of ours if you dare to dream  and you believe in yourself you can do  or be anything you want to be  and if we were able to accomplish what  we were able to accomplish in 1920 with  far less resources what are we capable  of now  i think the legacy honestly is that we  are more powerful  than we know they had no idea they were  making history they didn't care  about making history they just wanted to  play ball  [Music]  you

